		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Physics Programmer at Rockstar Games]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=24344#p24344"><![CDATA[
<strong class="text-strong">Rockstar Toronto</strong> is currently looking for a Physics Programmer to help us develop exciting physical simulations using our world class proprietary game engine. This programmer will be responsible for the game-specific application of physics elements such as ragdolls, biomechanical animation, vehicle dynamics, cloth, fragments, collisions, contact forces, fluid-dynamics, etc. We urge you to apply if you are seriously into games and want to work with the best in the industry. <br><br><strong class="text-strong">Required:</strong><br>    3+ years of game programming experience with an emphasis on physics<br>    Experience on Xbox360 or PS3<br>    Excellent C++ skills<br>    Superior 3D math skills<br><br><strong class="text-strong">Desired:</strong><br>    Experience with commercial game physics SDKs<br>    Experience writing physics simulation code<br>    Knowledge of game physics algorithms<br>    Experience with multithreaded/SPU programming<br>    Credit on a shipped AAA game<br>    Passion for playing and making great games<br>    Bachelor's Degree in Computer Science, Engineering, or Physics<br><br>Rockstar offers a competitive salary and benefits package and a great work environment.  <br>This is a full-time onsite position.  <br>Please go to <strong class="text-strong"><a href="http://jobs.rockstargames.com/" class="postlink">http://jobs.rockstargames.com/</a></strong><br>Select the red logo (Toronto), and submit your resume and cover letter from there.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=8439">RockstarGames</a> — Wed Jul 20, 2011 8:12 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
